[THOUGHT] CRP was requested but no result returned — likely unavailable or pending. To avoid further delay and stay within turn limits, I must select the next most discriminating *available* test. ESR is highly correlated with CRP in inflammatory knee conditions and is consistently listed as available. While less specific, ESR remains clinically useful:  
- In transient synovitis, ESR is often mildly elevated (20–40 mm/hr) or normal;  
- In early JIA, ESR may be normal initially but tends to rise with disease persistence;  
- A markedly elevated ESR (>40 mm/hr) would prompt concern for infection or systemic inflammation, while a normal ESR *plus* absence of clinical signs makes both transient synovitis and JIA possible — but favors transient synovitis given the benign, self-limited context.  

ESR is reliably available and provides actionable stratification.

[TEST] Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)

[THOUGHT] Repeated requests for CRP and ESR yielded no results — likely indicating these tests are not available in this environment (despite prior listing), or the system cannot retrieve them. I must pivot to the next most informative *available* test that discriminates between transient synovitis and early oligoarticular JIA. Among remaining available tests:  
- CBC (complete blood count) — can reveal leukocytosis (suggesting infection/inflammation) or anemia of chronic disease (more typical in persistent JIA);  
- ANA — highly relevant for oligoarticular JIA (positive in ~70–80% of cases), and a positive result significantly increases pretest probability, especially in a young girl (though gender not yet confirmed);  
- RF — typically negative in oligoarticular JIA, so low yield;  
- HLA-B27 — associated with spondyloarthropathies, not fitting this presentation.  

ANA is the most specific *serologic* marker for oligoarticular JIA and has strong predictive value for chronicity and uveitis risk. A positive ANA would strongly support JIA over transient synovitis (which is ANA-negative), while a negative ANA does not rule out JIA but makes it less likely — especially in absence of other red flags. Thus, ANA is the best remaining discriminating test.

[TEST] Antinuclear antibody (ANA)

